{"id": "EUVD-2026-86924", "enisaUuid": "d925f77a-b390-30e8-9ab7-5eda413dedc2", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nstaging: fbtft: make dirty_lock IRQ-safe\n\nfbtft_mkdirty() can be reached from the fbcon rendering path while\nprocessing printk() in hardirq context. Meanwhile, dirty_lock is also\ntaken by fbtft_deferred_io() in workqueue context with local interrupts\nenabled.\n\nLockdep reports a possible IRQ lock inversion involving dirty_lock and\nconsole_owner. A hardirq can interrupt a CPU holding dirty_lock and\nenter the console rendering path, which can attempt to acquire\ndirty_lock again.\n\nThe following lockdep report was observed on an RK3566 system with\nCONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING enabled:\n\n  WARNING: possible irq lock inversion dependency detected\n  swapper/2/0 just changed the state of lock:\n  (console_owner){-...}-{0:0}\n  but this lock took another, HARDIRQ-unsafe lock in the past:\n  (&par->dirty_lock){+.+.}-{2:2}\n\n  CPU0                    CPU1\n  ----                    ----\n  lock(&par->dirty_lock);\n                         local_irq_disable();\n                         lock(console_owner);\n                         lock(&par->dirty_lock);\n  <Interrupt>\n    lock(console_owner);\n\n  *** DEADLOCK ***\n\nUse spin_lock_irqsave() for fbtft_mkdirty() and spin_lock_irq() for\nfbtft_deferred_io(). They only access the dirty line range, so the\nIRQ-off regions remain short.", "datePublished": "2026-09-25T10:24:25", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-19T09:13:46", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/0843298b159599ee7bc6d14800efdb4bc7349706", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a323066337ed85c38decfc7f6bbf42f27605dff1", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f2451e18826eb6a33ea51419f39bdd2eb99fa1ea", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/10cc145b873670438d3b105133dffdae70822db6", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/2a609e29efbba79f9abd68c4ec8a2bd7ecf291e7", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f0c869df2c33793c8828acaab3e4a5e0176f9f00", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/dcb48fde8003492256dee45815144aa5ed26ce7c", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f576944a59f31bcffff121117ebf452c5dd162b7"], "aliases": ["GHSA-5wg8-22v7-6q2c", "CVE-2026-98094"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.18, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "0a45797f-539f-3d1c-803a-df106f8ae223", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "c296d5f9957c03994a699d6739c27d4581a9f6c7 <f2451e18826eb6a33ea51419f39bdd2eb99fa1ea"}, {"id": "0e16d78f-2e0c-37f3-b462-fe1c5fd6cf67", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "c296d5f9957c03994a699d6739c27d4581a9f6c7 <0843298b159599ee7bc6d14800efdb4bc7349706"}, {"id": "11314e0b-cc73-3616-8d0d-76313c727fea", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.158"}, {"id": "1f017fda-b991-31b1-9e93-22ded0371671", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.53"}, {"id": "2a805edd-4b87-30e0-8221-d86634873e0d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.3"}, {"id": "44983134-afea-3d9d-9394-9a9559fcb981", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "c296d5f9957c03994a699d6739c27d4581a9f6c7 <2a609e29efbba79f9abd68c4ec8a2bd7ecf291e7"}, {"id": "4d17545c-3981-39fe-a08e-9f1d4a4300e9", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.111"}, {"id": "4d3aa050-c5eb-37b3-8b75-3b89288ab90f", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "c296d5f9957c03994a699d6739c27d4581a9f6c7 <f576944a59f31bcffff121117ebf452c5dd162b7"}, {"id": "58253682-c491-3881-8da0-02c7b8b66ca7", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.10.271"}, {"id": "75ef7d2e-e14c-3b2a-afe3-2ef837681248", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.1.1